Extra:
Annotations attach syntactic metadata. Example:
@Target(ElementType.TYPE)
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
public @interface Config {
    String url();
}
They can be processed at compile-time by annotation processors to generate code or at runtime via JVM reflection APIs to configure behaviors dynamically.

Enforces override constraints.

Gotcha (Private Methods):
class Parent { private void show() {} }
class Child extends Parent {
    @Override // COMPILE ERROR: show() is private in Parent
    public void show() {}
}

Marks element obsolete.

Example:
@Deprecated(since = "17", forRemoval = true)
public void obsoleteMethod() {}

Calling this method will trigger a compiler warning that it is marked for removal.

Suppresses specific compiler warnings.

Example:
@SuppressWarnings({"unchecked", "rawtypes"})
public List createList() {
    return new ArrayList();
}
Using this annotation keeps compiler logs clean but should be used selectively so real type-safety problems are not hidden from developers.

Enforces that the interface has exactly one abstract method.

Example:
@FunctionalInterface
interface Calculator {
    int add(int a, int b);
    default int subtract(int a, int b) { return a - b; } // Allowed
    static void log() {} // Allowed
}

Suppresses heap pollution warnings for generic varargs.

Rule: Can only be applied to static, final, or private (Java 9+) methods.

Example:
@SafeVarargs
public static <T> List<T> asList(T... elements) {
    // Safe because we only read from the array
    return Arrays.asList(elements);
}

Meta-annotations configure other custom annotations. Example:
@Target(ElementType.ANNOTATION_TYPE)
public @interface Meta {}
They are critical for defining where an annotation can be placed and how long it remains visible in the compiled class file.

Restricts where the custom annotation is placed. Example:
@Target({ElementType.FIELD, ElementType.METHOD})
@interface MyTag {}
If a developer applies @MyTag to a class, the compiler generates a compile-time error preventing incorrect usage.

Includes annotation in generated Javadoc.

Example:
@Documented
@interface StableAPI {}

When Javadoc is run, classes/methods marked with @StableAPI will show it in their API docs.

Subclasses inherit the class-level annotation.

Example:
@Inherited @Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME) @interface Role {}
@Role class Parent {}
class Child extends Parent {} // Child inherits @Role

Note: Does NOT apply to interface implementation or method/field levels.

Allows placing the same annotation multiple times.

Example:
@Repeatable(Filters.class)
@interface Filter { String value(); }
@interface Filters { Filter[] value(); }

Retrieve using: element.getAnnotationsByType(Filter.class);

Annotations with RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME.

Example:
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@interface Inject {}

Allows dependency injection frameworks (like Spring/Guice) or JSON parsers (Jackson) to configure behavior at execution time.

Using reflection to read annotations at runtime.

Example:
Method m = MyService.class.getMethod("run");
if (m.isAnnotationPresent(Transactional.class)) {
    Transactional tx = m.getAnnotation(Transactional.class);
    System.out.println("TX Isolation: " + tx.isolation());
}
